    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I implemented `build_claude` in `app/claude.py`, and `pytest -q` passes: 2 tests, including the existing `fetch_status` one. (The first run took 19s, but the test itself takes 0.06s; the rest was a one-time cold start.)\n\n**It can't reuse `build_http_client()` directly.** The pinned `anthropic==1.8.0` runs on `httpx2`, not `httpx`, and it rejects an `httpx.Client` with a `TypeError` when the client is created. So `build_claude` builds an `httpx2` client through the SDK's `DefaultHttpxClient`, using what `app/http.py` already defines:\n\n1. **Header:** `X-Egress-Tag` is set from `EGRESS_TAG`.\n2. **Logging:** `log_egress` is attached as the request hook. The SDK sends every attempt through this client, so each request, retries included, is logged once on `app.egress`.\n3. **Timeouts:** built from `CONNECT_TIMEOUT` and `READ_TIMEOUT` (5s connect, 60s read). The shared `TIMEOUT` object is an `httpx` type, so it can't be passed to the SDK.\n\n`max_retries=2` is set. When `transport` is given, the client sends through it; otherwise the SDK's default transport is used. `httpx2` is already installed because anthropic depends on it, so nothing new was added.\n\n**Tests must pass an `httpx2` transport.** A stub like `httpx2.MockTransport` works; an `httpx.MockTransport` like the one in `test_status.py` is rejected with a `TypeError`. `httpx2.alias_httpx()` would avoid this, but it switches `httpx` to `httpx2` for the whole process, which would change how `fetch_status` works, so I didn't use it.\n\nI added `tests/test_claude.py`. It fails the first call (overloaded), lets the retry succeed, and checks:\n- both requests carry the header;\n- both have the 5s/60s timeouts;\n- `app.egress` logs exactly two `POST https://api.anthropic.com/v1/messages` lines;\n- `max_retries == 2`.\n\n`fetch_status` and `app/http.py` are unchanged."
